+# Implemented architecture
+
+This document describes the current source layout on `feature/refactoring`. It is
+about the implementation, not an earlier planned package layout.
+
+## Production boundary
+
+Production Java code lives below `src/main/java/compiler/`. The five
+architectural modules are:
+
+| Module | Package | Responsibility |
+|---|---|---|
+| Lexer | `compiler.lexer` | Reads characters, recognizes tokens, normalizes lexical forms, and validates literal representations. |
+| Parser | `compiler.parser` | Implements the FCL grammar, coordinates lexical input, performs grammar-directed semantic actions, and publishes declarations and metadata. |
+| AST | `compiler.ast` | Represents the rule-block syntax tree consumed by lowering. |
+| Generator | `compiler.generator` | Owns the target controller model, fuzzy evaluation/defuzzification abstractions, and C++ generation. |
+| Repository | `compiler.repository` | Holds shared symbol metadata and diagnostics used across compilation phases. |
+
+`compiler.FlcCompiler` is the pipeline facade that composes these modules;
+it is not a sixth compiler phase. `compiler.GeneratedFile` represents an
+in-memory generated artifact.
+
+## Compilation flow
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+    FCL["FCL source"] --> LEX["compiler.lexer"]
+    LEX --> PAR["compiler.parser"]
+    PAR --> AST["compiler.ast<br/>rule-block AST"]
+    PAR <--> REP["compiler.repository<br/>symbols and diagnostics"]
+    AST --> LOW["generator.lowers<br/>lowering"]
+    REP --> LOW
+    LOW --> MODEL["ControllerModel"]
+    MODEL --> GEN["generator code emission"]
+    GEN --> CPP["Vivado HLS C++"]
+    COMP["FlcCompiler"] -. orchestrates .-> LEX
+    COMP -. orchestrates .-> PAR
+    COMP -. orchestrates .-> LOW
+    COMP -. orchestrates .-> GEN
+```
+
+The diagram is conceptual: the parser also consumes the lexer, and compilation
+phases share repository state. `FlcCompiler` wires the concrete objects and
+controls the sequence.
+
+## Repository is an architectural component
+
+`compiler.repository` is not a generic utilities package. It is the shared
+semantic repository of the compiler.
+
+* `SymbolTable` indexes `LexemeInfo` records by lexeme name.
+* `LexemeInfo` carries the semantic attributes accumulated for declarations
+  (type, subtype, use, source, limits, parameters and initialization).
+* Builders and `Director` recipes centralize construction of those records.
+* `DiagnosticsHandler` collects errors and warnings and exposes whether
+  compilation has fatal errors.
+* `enums` and `diagnostics` define the vocabulary used by the repository and
+  its clients.
+
+The lexer and parser publish information; later phases query it. This shared
+repository avoids forcing later phases to reconstruct declarations from token
+or parser-local state. It is an explicit dependency and a design trade-off:
+phases are decoupled from the concrete publication sequence, but they remain
+coupled to the repository's semantic model and lifecycle. It should not be
+described as a database or persistence layer.
+
+## Lowering belongs to Generator
+
+Lowering is implemented under `compiler.generator.lowers`; it is not a
+top-level production module. `ControllerLower` is the entry point. It
+transforms the parser's outputs (the shared symbol table and rule-block AST)
+into a `ControllerModel`, which is the input contract of code generation.
+
+Lowering also checks whether the parsed/controller features can be represented
+by the current generator. Unsupported features are reported as compiler
+diagnostics rather than silently omitted. The separation is useful because
+the parser models source-language declarations and syntax, while the model
+represents the subset and shape that the target generator can emit.
+
+## Test-only support
+
+The simulation helpers under `src/test/java/utils/simulation` are test support,
+not a production module and not part of the generated application. See
+[SIMULATION.md](SIMULATION.md) for their assumptions, value and limits.
+
+## Source of truth
+
+When updating this page, inspect `src/main/java/compiler/` first. Do not infer
+package boundaries from historical names such as `utils`, `codegen`,
+`lowering`, or a top-level `simulation` package.
